{
  "dom_id": "step-q314895",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "An environmental researcher is tracking the depth $d$, in meters, of a lake during a dry season. The following table shows the rate at which the depth is decreasing.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(weeks)}$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${d}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{(meters/week)}$ | $-0.3$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.8$ | $-1.0$ |\n\nAt $t=2,$ the lake depth was $12$ meters. Use the tangent line approximation of $d$ at time $t=2$ to estimate the depth of the lake at $t=2.4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nmeters.",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $d(t)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =d(a)+{d}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =d(2)+{d}^{′}(2)(t-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $d(2.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=2.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(2.4) & ≈L(2.4) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(2.4-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(0.4) \\\\  & =12-0.2 \\\\  & =11.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be $11.8$ meters.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
